  "title": "Mirror Neurons: Learning by Watching",
  "emoji": "⚡",
  "color": "#8B5CF6",
  "description": "A 60-second lesson on Mirror Neurons: Learning by Watching.",
  "is_free": false,
  "published": true,
  "story": {
    "hook": {
      "text": "A monkey watches a researcher pick up a peanut. The same neurons fire as if the monkey did it itself.",
      "visual": "🐒"
    },
    "buildup": {
      "text": "Mirror neurons activate both when you perform an action and when you observe someone else doing it.",
      "visual": "🪞"
    },
    "discovery": {
      "text": "Discovered by accident in the 1990s, they may underlie imitation, empathy, and language learning.",
      "visual": "🗣️"
    },
    "twist": {
      "text": "Some researchers think mirror neuron dysfunction contributes to social difficulties in autism.",
      "visual": "🧩"
    },
    "climax": {
      "text": "Others argue their role has been exaggerated—the debate is one of neuroscience's liveliest.",
      "visual": "🔥"
    },
    "punchline": {
      "text": "Mirror neurons suggest your brain rehearses what it sees others do.",
      "visual": "🧠"
    }
  },
  "quiz": {
    "question": "When do mirror neurons fire?",
    "options": [
      "When performing or observing an action",
      "Only during sleep",
      "Only when feeling pain"
    ],
    "correct": 0
  },
